<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<section xml:id="sec-z-scores">
  <title>Z-Scores and Probability </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We will learn how z-scores can be used to evaluate how extreme a given value is within a particular set or population. 
      By connecting these standard values to probability density distributions, you will develop the skills necessary to calculate the probability of a data point falling between two specified z-scores, as well as the ability to work backward to determine an exact z-score when given a target probability threshold.
    </p>
    <p>
      Using the Empirical Rule can give you a good idea of the probability of occurrence of a value that happens to be exactly one, two, or three standard deviations to either side of the mean.
      But how do you compare the probabilities of values that are in between those standard deviations?
    </p>
    <p>
      Z-scores are closely related to the Empirical Rule; both offer methods for evaluating how extreme a particular value is within a given dataset.
      You can think of a z-score as the exact number of standard deviations there are between a given value and the mean of the set.
      While the Empirical Rule only allows you to associate the first three integer standard deviations with fixed percentages (<m>68\%</m>, <m>95\%</m>, and <m>99.7\%</m>), the z-score allows you to state, with arbitrary precision, exactly how many standard deviations a value lies above or below the mean.
    </p>
    <p>
      Conceptually, the calculation is straightforward.
      To find the number of standard deviations between a value and the mean, first calculate the difference between your specific data point and the population mean.
      Then, divide that difference by the standard deviation of the dataset.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <definition xml:id="def-z-score-formula">
    <title>Z-Score Formula</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The mathematical formula to calculate a standard z-score is:
        <me>z = \frac{\text{value} - \text{mean}}{\text{standard deviation}} = \frac{x - \mu}{\sigma}</me>
        Where <m>x</m> is the data value, <m>\mu</m> is the population mean, and <m>\sigma</m> is the standard deviation.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    For example, with <m>\mu = 65</m> and <m>\sigma = 3</m>, a score of <m>59</m> gives:
    <me>z = \frac{59 - 65}{3} = -2</me>
    This means the value is 2 standard deviations <em>below</em> the mean.
  </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-pos-z">
      <title>Positive Z-score</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\mu = 24</m> and <m>\sigma = 2</m>. Find the z-score for <m>x = 27</m>.
          <me>z = \frac{27 - 24}{2} = 1.5</me>
          The value is <m>1.5</m> standard deviations <em>above</em> the mean.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-neg-z">
      <title>Negative Z-score</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\mu = 125</m> and <m>\sigma = 6.2</m>. Find the z-score for <m>x = 104.5</m>.
          <me>z = \frac{104.5 - 125}{6.2} \approx -3.31</me>
          The resulting value is approximately <m>3.31</m> standard deviations <em>below</em> the mean.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-reverse-z">
      <title>Finding a Value Given a Z-score</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the original raw data value <m>x</m> if the mean is <m>63</m>, the standard deviation is <m>4.25</m>, and the given z-score is <m>2.403</m>.
          <me>2.403 = \frac{x - 63}{4.25}</me>
          Multiplying both sides by <m>4.25</m> and adding <m>63</m> yields:
          <me>x = 63 + (2.403)(4.25) \approx 73.21</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

<insight xml:id="insight-2-14-34-rule">
  <title>The 2-14-34 Rule</title>
  <p>
    A helpful tool for quick mental approximations of these probabilities is the <term>2-14-34 Rule</term>. 
    Because a normal distribution curve is completely symmetric, either half of the bell curve extending outward from the center mean (<m>z=0</m>) can be broken down into specific operational percentage blocks:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><p><m>34\%</m> of the population data falls between <m>z=0</m> and <m>z=1</m> (or between <m>z=0</m> and <m>z=-1</m>).</p></li>
    <li><p><m>14\%</m> of the population data falls between <m>z=1</m> and <m>z=2</m> (or between <m>z=-1</m> and <m>z=-2</m>).</p></li>
    <li><p><m>2\%</m> of the population data falls between <m>z=2</m> and <m>z=3</m> (or between <m>z=-2</m> and <m>z=-3</m>).</p></li>
  </ul>
</insight>

<figure xml:id="fig-normal-d-curve">
  <caption>The 2-14-34 Rule Area Distribution under a Standard Normal Curve</caption>
  <image xml:id="img-norm-curve" width="85%">
    <description>A bell-shaped normal distribution curve divided into symmetric shaded segments labeled 0.02, 0.14, and 0.34 extending outward from the center z equals 0 to z equals 3.</description>
    <latex-image>
      \begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1.6, yscale=1.7]
        % Base Shading Zones using static coordinates to protect the parser
        % Zone -3 to -2 (2%)
        \fill[blue!10] (-3,0) -- (-3,0.05) to[out=5,in=170] (-2,0.2) -- (-2,0) -- cycle;
        % Zone -2 to -1 (14%)
        \fill[blue!20] (-2,0) -- (-2,0.2) to[out=10,in=240] (-1,0.9) -- (-1,0) -- cycle;
        % Zone -1 to 0 (34%)
        \fill[blue!30] (-1,0) -- (-1,0.9) to[out=60,in=180] (0,2.3) -- (0,0) -- cycle;
        % Zone 0 to 1 (34%)
        \fill[blue!30] (0,0) -- (0,2.3) to[out=0,in=120] (1,0.9) -- (1,0) -- cycle;
        % Zone 1 to 2 (14%)
        \fill[blue!20] (1,0) -- (1,0.9) to[out=300,in=170] (2,0.2) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
        % Zone 2 to 3 (2%)
        \fill[blue!10] (2,0) -- (2,0.2) to[out=350,in=175] (3,0.05) -- (3,0) -- cycle;

        % Outer Frame and Smooth Boundary Curve
        \draw[line width=1.5pt] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0);
        \draw[line width=1.5pt] (-3.5,0.01) 
        to[out=0,in=180] (-3,0.05) 
        to[out=5,in=190] (-2,0.2) 
        to[out=10,in=240] (-1,0.9) 
        to[out=60,in=180] (0,2.3) 
        to[out=0,in=120] (1,0.9) 
        to[out=300,in=170] (2,0.2) 
        to[out=350,in=175] (3,0.05) 
        to[out=355,in=180] (3.5,0.01);

        % Vertical Boundary Gridlines
        \draw[dashed, opacity=0.6] (-3,0) -- (-3,0.05);
        \draw[dashed, opacity=0.6] (-2,0) -- (-2,0.2);
        \draw[dashed, opacity=0.6] (-1,0) -- (-1,0.9);
        \draw[dashed, opacity=0.6] (0,0) -- (0,2.3);
        \draw[dashed, opacity=0.6] (1,0) -- (1,0.9);
        \draw[dashed, opacity=0.6] (2,0) -- (2,0.2);
        \draw[dashed, opacity=0.6] (3,0) -- (3,0.05);

        % Z-Score X-Axis Ticks
        \node[below] at (-3,-0.1) {$z=-3$};
        \node[below] at (-2,-0.1) {$z=-2$};
        \node[below] at (-1,-0.1) {$z=-1$};
        \node[below] at (0,-0.1) {$z=0$};
        \node[below] at (1,-0.1) {$z=1$};
        \node[below] at (2,-0.1) {$z=2$};
        \node[below] at (3,-0.1) {$z=3$};

        % Probability Labels
        \node at (-2.5,0.3) {\small 0.02};
        \node at (-1.5,0.5) {\small 0.14};
        \node at (-0.5,1.1) {\small 0.34};
        \node at (0.5,1.1)  {\small 0.34};
        \node at (1.5,0.5)  {\small 0.14};
        \node at (2.5,0.3)  {\small 0.02};
      \end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
  </image>
</figure>


    <example xml:id="ex-rule-between">
      <title>Finding Area Between Specific Bounds</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the 2-14-34 Rule, determine the approximate percentage of data that falls between a z-score of <m>z = -1</m> and a z-score of <m>z = 2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We break the region down into its individual standardized blocks from left to right:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>The area between <m>z = -1</m> and <m>z = 0</m> contains <m>34\%</m> of the data.</p></li>
          <li><p>The area between <m>z = 0</m> and <m>z = 1</m> contains <m>34\%</m> of the data.</p></li>
          <li><p>The area between <m>z = 1</m> and <m>z = 2</m> contains <m>14\%</m> of the data.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Summing these continuous regions together gives us:
          <me>\text{Total Area} = 34\% + 34\% + 14\% = 82\%</me>
          Therefore, approximately <m>82\%</m> of the population values fall within this score boundary.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

<example xml:id="ex-rule-greater-than">
      <title>Calculating Tail Probabilities</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A standardized industrial process yield results in a normal curve distribution. 
          Using the 2-14-34 Rule, approximate the probability that a randomly chosen sample yields a z-score greater than <m>z = 1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Because the normal curve is perfectly symmetric, exactly <m>50\%</m> of all data lies above the center mean (<m>z = 0</m>).
          To find the portion greater than <m>z = 1</m>, we subtract the region we do not want from the total right half:
          <me>P(z \gt 1) = 50\% - \text{Area}(z=0 \text{ to } z=1)</me>
          <me>P(z \gt 1) = 50\% - 34\% = 16\%</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Alternatively, you can get the exact same answer by summing the individual blocks extending to the right of <m>z = 1</m>:
          <me>\text{Area}(z=1 \text{ to } z=2) + \text{Area}(z=2 \text{ to } z=3) = 14\% + 2\% = 16\%</me>
          Thus, there is roughly a <m>16\%</m> probability that a random value has a z-score greater than <m>1</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>



  <exercises xml:id="exercises-z-scores">
    <title>Practice Exercises</title>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A retail store calculates that the mean price for a specific model of skis is <m>\mu = \$279</m> with a standard deviation of <m>\sigma = \$16</m>. 
          Find the z-score for a pair of skis priced at <m>\$247</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <me>z = \frac{247 - 279}{16} = -2</me>
          The price is exactly <m>2</m> standard deviations below the mean.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The mean number of ice cream scoops served per order at a shop is <m>3</m> scoops with a standard deviation of <m>1</m> scoop.
          Find the z-score for an order containing <m>5</m> scoops.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <me>z = \frac{5 - 3}{1} = 2</me>
          The order size is <m>2</m> standard deviations above the mean.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The mean weight of a specific cattle breed population is <m>\mu = 1150</m> lbs with a standard deviation of <m>\sigma = 77</m> lbs.
          Calculate the z-score for a cow that weighs <m>825</m> lbs.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <me>z = \frac{825 - 1150}{77} \approx -4.22</me>
          The cow's weight is approximately <m>4.22</m> standard deviations below the mean.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute the z-score for a sample measurement of <m>x = 0.0034</m> taken from a population where <m>\mu = 0.0041</m> and <m>\sigma = 0.0008</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <me>z = \frac{0.0034 - 0.0041}{0.0008} = -0.875</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          On a standardized certification exam, the mean score is <m>76</m> with a standard deviation of <m>5</m>.
          Determine the z-score for an examinee who scored an <m>88</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <me>z = \frac{88 - 76}{5} = 2.4</me>
          This student scored <m>2.4</m> standard deviations above the population average.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A track-and-field sprint group has a mean race completion time of <m>24.5</m> seconds with a standard deviation of <m>3.2</m> seconds.
          What is the z-score for a runner who completes the race in <m>19.1</m> seconds?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <me>z = \frac{19.1 - 24.5}{3.2} \approx -1.69</me>
          The runner's time is <m>1.69</m> standard deviations faster (below) than the group average.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An industrial lighting brand has a mean runtime life of <m>15,000</m> hours with a standard deviation of <m>850</m> hours.
          Find the specific operational lifespan <m>x</m> that corresponds to a z-score of <m>-1.40</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <me>x = \mu + z\sigma</me>
          <me>x = 15000 + (-1.40)(850) = 13810\text{ hours}</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>








</section>